## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. Change is determined by `===` comparison — **not deep equality**.

Primitives compare by value (`"hello" === "hello"` → true), but objects and functions compare by reference. Every render creates a new reference:

```jsx
// New object reference on every render of Parent
<Child config={{ theme: "dark" }} />

// New function reference on every render of Parent
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Even if the values are identical, `{} !== {}` and `() => {} !== () => {}`, so the child always sees "new" props and re-renders.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component to skip re-rendering if props haven't changed (shallow comparison). Useless if you're passing unstable references.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, config }) => { ... });
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders. Use it when passing callbacks to memoized children or as effect dependencies.

```jsx
// Stable reference — only recreated when `id` changes
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value or object. Use it for expensive calculations, or to stabilize object references passed as props.

```jsx
// Stable reference — only recreated when `theme` changes
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme, dense: true }), [theme]);
```

**When NOT to use them:** Don't reach for these by default. They add overhead (memory + comparison cost). Only memoize when you've identified a real re-render problem, or when a child is genuinely expensive to render.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

The most common mistake: passing a new object literal as the context value.

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every render of App, all consumers re-render
<UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
```

Every component consuming this context re-renders whenever `App` re-renders, even if `user` and `setUser` haven't changed.

**Fix:** memoize the value, or split into separate contexts (one for data, one for dispatch).

```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user, setUser]);
<UserContext.Provider value={value}>
```

Splitting contexts is often better — components that only call `setUser` won't re-render when `user` changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before — three problems at once:**

```jsx
function ProductList({ category }) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

  // New function reference every render
  const fetchItems = async () => {
    const data = await api.get(category);
    setItems(data);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {items.map(item => (
        // New object prop + new handler every render
        <ProductCard
          key={item.id}
          item={item}
          style={{ padding: 8 }}           // new object
          onSelect={() => select(item.id)} // new function
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

const ProductCard = ({ item, style, onSelect }) => { ... };
```

**After — stable references, memoized child:**

```jsx
const CARD_STYLE = { padding: 8 }; // stable: defined outside component

function ProductList({ category }) {
  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);

  const fetchItems = useCallback(async () => {
    const data = await api.get(category);
    setItems(data);
  }, [category]); // recreated only when category changes

  return (
    <div>
      {items.map(item => (
        <ProductCard
          key={item.id}
          item={item}
          style={CARD_STYLE}
          onSelect={item.onSelect} // pre-bound, or use useCallback per item
        />
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}

// Skips re-render if item, style, onSelect references are unchanged
const ProductCard = React.memo(({ item, style, onSelect }) => { ... });
```

---

**Rule of thumb:** profile first (`React DevTools` → Profiler tab), then fix the hot spots. Premature memoization adds noise and rarely helps.
